window.X_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Home","url":"index.html","description":"","text":"Designers lead Designers set the direction, make the anchor screens and decide at both gates. Agents extend and check Agents do the work between the gates: they explore, extend from the anchors, check and hand over. People review what they produce. Buckholt never changes Agents build with Buckholt as it is. Anything Buckholt does not have is a flagged extension, registered for review."},{"title":"Agents","url":"agents/index.html","description":"","text":"Archive Keeps design context accurate and the same in the repo and SharePoint. Brief Turns a product feature into a UX problem and chooses the route. Insight Synthesises research and behaviour evidence. Map Turns findings into structure and a design worklist. Range Generates many quick, deliberately rough directions. Critic Stress tests the explorations before the shortlist gate. Extend Builds out the design from the designers' anchor screens. Align Checks consistency across all Figma work, designers' work included. Build Turns signed off designs into prototypes. Audit Runs automated quality checks before people see a prototype. Observe Supports usability testing around the sessions. Relay Hands over to engineering, then checks what was built."},{"title":"Archive","url":"agents/archive/index.html","description":"Keeps design context accurate and the same in the repo and SharePoint.","text":"Keeps design context accurate and the same in the repo and SharePoint. Cursor, plus a weekly scheduled run A design item reaching Discovery approved A merged change to /context The weekly check /context SharePoint discovery site Figma exports Azure DevOps design items On Discovery approved, write /context/discovery/ feature .md with the key findings, worklist and links to the full SharePoint documents. After a product pack change is merged, update its SharePoint copy. After each gate, add the decision to decision log.md from the gate notes. Weekly, flag packs older than 90 days, register entries missing their Figma or code half, and gates with no decision logged. Track daily Figma MCP calls and warn the team at 80% of the limit. Updated /context files SharePoint product pack copies Weekly health report Laurence or Jon approves every product pack change. Edit /buckholt or /legacy Change a pack without approval Delete history Repo and SharePoint copies match and the health report is posted."},{"title":"Brief","url":"agents/brief/index.html","description":"Turns a product feature into a UX problem and chooses the route.","text":"Turns a product feature into a UX problem and chooses the route. Microsoft 365 Copilot A feature set to Ready for design Azure DevOps feature and stories SharePoint specs Product pack copy Related Teams and Outlook threads Check the feature has a problem, outcome, products, users, priority, quarter and constraints. If not, comment the gaps on the feature and stop. Find the products and styling modes in the pack copies. Stop if a pack is missing. Choose the route. Fast track only if there is no new flow, no new research is needed and only existing screens change. Otherwise full. Record why. Write the UX brief from the template. Draft success measures and request baselines from the Insight LogRocket step. Save to SharePoint and link it on the design item. UX brief The PM confirms scope; a designer moves the status on. Rewrite the PM's feature or stories Invent baselines Start without a product pack The brief is linked and the PM has confirmed scope."},{"title":"Insight","url":"agents/insight/index.html","description":"Synthesises research and behaviour evidence.","text":"Synthesises research and behaviour evidence. Microsoft 365 Copilot, plus a Cursor step for LogRocket Status Discovery SharePoint research Teams transcripts Lucid boards UX brief LogRocket Cursor step List every source used. Anonymise names, emails, account numbers and any customer details. Group findings into themes, each with its evidence and product tag. Rank pain points by how often and how badly they occur. Cursor step: pull LogRocket baselines for the success measures completion, drop off, errors, rage clicks on the flows in scope. List evidence gaps and the research that would fill them. Save the discovery summary to SharePoint. Discovery summary with baselines Designers validate the insights. Present assumptions as findings Keep personal data Generalise from one user The summary is saved and every theme cites evidence."},{"title":"Map","url":"agents/map/index.html","description":"Turns findings into structure and a design worklist.","text":"Turns findings into structure and a design worklist. Microsoft 365 Copilot and Lucid The discovery summary being saved Discovery summary Product pack copy Existing Lucid flows Map the current journey in Lucid. Propose the future journey, or two options if the evidence splits. Write the screen inventory: new, changed and unchanged screens. Flag any clash with the product's navigation or patterns. Write the design worklist: screens, states and edge cases to design. Save to Lucid and SharePoint. Journey maps Screen inventory Design worklist Designers approve, then move the status to Discovery approved. Break a product's navigation pattern without flagging it Flows and worklist are approved."},{"title":"Range","url":"agents/range/index.html","description":"Generates many quick, deliberately rough directions.","text":"Generates many quick, deliberately rough directions. Cursor, sandbox only Discovery approved on a full route item /context/discovery/ feature .md Design worklist Patterns from other products Lucid flows Write 3 or 4 \"how might we\" prompts from the worklist. A designer may replace them. Generate 5 to 10 directions, including at least two that break the current pattern and opposing pairs guided vs expert, minimal vs dense . Build each as clickable greybox HTML in /sandbox/ product / feature / using greybox.css only. Add an \"Exploration, not for build\" banner to every page. Write an index page: each direction, its one line idea and trade offs. Post the index link on the design item. Sandbox explorations Index page Use Buckholt, brand colours or tokens Write outside the sandbox Polish The index is posted."},{"title":"Critic","url":"agents/critic/index.html","description":"Stress-tests the explorations before the shortlist gate.","text":"Stress tests the explorations before the shortlist gate. Cursor The Range index being posted Sandbox explorations /context/discovery/ feature .md Product rules For each direction, assess user needs: which themes it serves and which it misses. Check edge cases: empty, error, loading, permissions, heavy data, long text. Check consistency with the product's patterns and styling mode. Estimate build effort low, medium or high with the reason. List risks. Recommend up to three directions, labelled as a recommendation. critique.md in the sandbox feature folder, linked on the design item The shortlist gate designers, PM, engineer decides. Make the decision Judge taste or brand Every direction has a critique."},{"title":"Extend","url":"agents/extend/index.html","description":"Builds out the design from the designers' anchor screens.","text":"Builds out the design from the designers' anchor screens. Cursor + Figma MCP the only agent with write access Status Anchors ready Frames labelled Anchor The product's Figma library Design worklist Figma usage log Read the anchors and list the layout rules, components, spacing and text styles used. Work through the worklist in batches of up to 5 screens. Use library components as instances; never detach them. Add every state and edge case on the worklist. If a component is missing, build it on the Extensions page with an x/ prefix and add a register entry. After each batch, leave a note: what was built and what was assumed. Apply feedback changes when asked. Remaining screens States and variants Flagged components Designers review every batch. Detach or change library components Edit anchors Work without anchors The worklist is covered and Align has run."},{"title":"Align","url":"agents/align/index.html","description":"Checks consistency across all Figma work, designers' work included.","text":"Checks consistency across all Figma work, designers' work included. Cursor + Figma MCP read only Each Extend batch A designer's request Weekly on active files The named Figma pages only The product library The styling mode The extensions register Check for detached instances and styles not from the library. Check spacing and sizes against the token scale. Check for wrong or one off text styles. Check wording against the product pack's terms. Check for styling mode mismatches, such as Buckholt parts in a legacy screen. Check extension components are flagged and register entries have both halves. Issue list by severity with frame links, posted on the design item Designers decide what is a mistake and what is deliberate. Fix anything Change files The report is posted."},{"title":"Build","url":"agents/build/index.html","description":"Turns signed-off designs into prototypes.","text":"Turns signed off designs into prototypes. Cursor Status Design signed off Figma frames read only The product pack /buckholt or /legacy /extensions The prototype build spec Create /prototypes/ product / feature /. Buckholt mode: use Buckholt HTML and CSS. Legacy mode: use the legacy CSS. Hybrid: follow the pack, with every Buckholt element labelled. For a missing component, reuse a register entry if one exists; otherwise build it in /extensions/ product / with tokens only, an x prefix, data buckholt=\"false\" and a register entry. Wire the interactions the test tasks need. Publish the preview and post the link on the design item. Prototype Extensions Preview link Designers check fidelity and behaviour. Write to /buckholt or /legacy Import from /sandbox Hard code values Edit Figma The preview is posted and Audit has passed."},{"title":"Audit","url":"agents/audit/index.html","description":"Runs automated quality checks before people see a prototype.","text":"Runs automated quality checks before people see a prototype. Cursor + Playwright + axe Any new or changed prototype The prototype /tests The Buckholt HTML/CSS reference or legacy CSS Run axe on every page. Check the keyboard path through each test task, focus order and visible focus. Check contrast and labels. Check markup and classes against the Buckholt reference or legacy CSS . Flag hard coded colours, spacing and fonts. Flag x components missing a register entry. Audit report by severity, posted on the design item Blocks testing when a critical accessibility issue is found. Fix code Check the sandbox No critical issues remain."},{"title":"Observe","url":"agents/observe/index.html","description":"Supports usability testing around the sessions.","text":"Supports usability testing around the sessions. Cursor Audit passing and sessions being booked The prototype Success measures Teams recordings LogRocket baselines Draft the test plan and script from the success measures and test tasks. After sessions, tag recordings by task, success, hesitation, error and quote. Summarise per task and compare with the LogRocket baselines. Write findings: issue, how many participants, severity, suggested change, and whether it goes back to Extend or Build. Test plan Tagged clips Findings report Designers run the sessions and decide what changes. Run sessions Treat one participant's comment as a finding Keep personal data Findings are posted and routed."},{"title":"Relay","url":"agents/relay/index.html","description":"Hands over to engineering, then checks what was built.","text":"Hands over to engineering, then checks what was built. Cursor + Azure DevOps MCP A designer marking testing complete Final Figma screens The prototype Buckholt Storybook The extensions register Write a spec per screen: states, edge cases, interactions and content. Map each element to a Storybook component or a flagged extension. Write UX acceptance criteria Given, When, Then for each engineering story. Add them to the stories as a linked UX section with Figma and prototype links, and set the predecessor link. Update Design status on the feature to Handed over. After release, compare the build with the design, and LogRocket data with the success measures. Post the report. Specs UX acceptance criteria Linked stories Release check Designers review before handoff; engineering reviews feasibility. Hand over anything not signed off Edit the PM's story text Change engineering statuses Every story has its UX section and the release check is posted."},{"title":"Workflow","url":"workflow/index.html","description":"Work moves through six stages and two gates. Agents do the work in each stage; designers, the PM and an engineer decide at the gates.","text":"The workflow at a glance Stages Each stage has its own agents. An agent starts when the design item reaches the status that triggers it, and stops at a human check. Stage Agents Where they run Foundation Archive Cursor, plus a weekly scheduled run Discover Brief Insight Map Microsoft 365 Copilot, with Lucid and a Cursor step for LogRocket Explore Range Critic Cursor, in the sandbox Design Extend Align Cursor and Figma MCP Prototype Build Audit Observe Cursor, with Playwright and axe Handoff Relay Cursor and the Azure DevOps MCP Between Explore and Design, designers make the anchor screens in Figma. Extend builds out from them and never works without them. Gates There are two gates. At each one, designers, the PM and an engineer decide together, using the evidence the agents have produced. Stage statuses The design item moves through these statuses in order. Some of them start an agent. Status What it starts Discovery Insight synthesises research and behaviour evidence. Discovery approved Archive writes the discovery summary into the repo. Range starts on full route items. Shortlist agreed Set when the shortlist gate passes. Designers make the anchor screens. Anchors ready Extend builds out the design from the anchors. Design signed off Set when the design sign off gate passes. Build starts the prototype. Handed over Set on the feature by Relay. Engineering stories are ready. Brief starts earlier, when product sets the feature to Ready for design. Map's output is approved by designers, who then move the status to Discovery approved. Fast track Brief chooses the route for every feature and records why. The fast track goes from Discover straight to Design, skipping Explore and the shortlist gate. Use the fast track only when all three are true: there is no new flow no new research is needed only existing screens change Otherwise the feature takes the full route. Test loop Prototypes are tested with people. Observe writes up the findings, and each finding says whether it goes back to Extend a design change or Build a prototype change . Designers decide what changes. The loop repeats until testing is complete; then Relay hands over. Cadence under scaled agile Design runs one sprint ahead of engineering: engineering stories are ready only when the design is Handed over. To come: The full cadence under scaled agile, including how design work lines up with planning increments, will be added from the source doc."},{"title":"Toolset","url":"toolset/index.html","description":"Discovery happens in Microsoft 365. Exploring, designing and prototyping happen in Cursor. Archive keeps the two in step.","text":"Two platforms and a bridge Microsoft 365 side Copilot runs Brief, Insight and Map. SharePoint holds the discovery master copy and a copy of each product pack. Teams holds recordings and transcripts. Azure DevOps holds the design board, the stage status and the links between work items. Lucid holds journey and process maps. It is also reached from Cursor. Cursor side Cursor runs the other nine agents. Figma MCP : Extend writes; Align, Build and Relay only read. LogRocket MCP is used by the Insight Cursor step, Observe and Relay. The Buckholt prototyping repo holds Buckholt core, extensions, the sandbox and the context Archive maintains. Playwright and axe run the automated checks for Audit. Lucid MCP is used by Range for rough flows. The bridge Archive keeps the two sides in step. It copies product packs into SharePoint after a change is merged, and pulls approved discovery into the repo as /context/discovery/ feature .md . Access needed Foundations come first, then connectors and access, then the agents. Access Who needs it Figma MCP, write Extend. Needs a Full seat. Figma read and Code Connect Align, Build, Relay Legacy Figma libraries Anchor screens, Extend, Align LogRocket MCP Insight, Observe, Relay Lucid MCP Map, Range Azure DevOps connector Brief, to read features Archive tracks daily Figma MCP calls and warns the team at 80% of the limit. The set up guides /setup guides/ take you through each one in order."},{"title":"Repo and Buckholt","url":"repo-and-buckholt/index.html","description":"Buckholt never changes. Agents create only in three folders, and everything they make outside Buckholt is flagged and registered.","text":"Repo zones Proposed layout: These folder names are the proposed layout for the prototyping repo. They are not all in place yet. Locks /buckholt holds Buckholt core and /legacy/ product holds captured legacy CSS. No agent writes to either. Changing them needs the owner's approval. Every agent that touches code is told so in its rules: Archive never edits them, Build never writes to them. Extensions When Buckholt has no component for what a design needs, it becomes an extension. In code, Build first reuses an existing register entry. If there is none, it builds the component in /extensions/ product / . In Figma, Extend builds it on the Extensions page with an x/ prefix. Every code extension follows the same rules: Buckholt tokens only CSS custom properties . No hard coded colours, spacing, radii or fonts. Class prefix x and the attribute data buckholt=\"false\" . One entry per component in the extensions register, with both halves: Figma and code. Align checks that extension components are flagged and register entries have both halves. Audit flags x components with no register entry. Archive flags register entries missing a half in its weekly check. html \"An extension, flagged\" ... Sandbox Range works only in /sandbox/ product / feature / . Explorations are clickable greybox HTML using greybox.css only, with an \"Exploration, not for build\" banner on every page. They never use Buckholt, brand colours or tokens. Nothing in the sandbox is ever promoted. Build never imports from it, and Audit does not check it. Styling modes Each product pack records the product's styling mode. Build follows it: Mode What Build uses Buckholt Buckholt HTML and CSS Legacy The product's legacy CSS Hybrid What the pack says, with every Buckholt element labelled Align checks for styling mode mismatches, such as Buckholt parts in a legacy screen. Rules files /agents holds one file per agent: its job, triggers, steps, limits and when it is done. Laurence writes them. /.cursor/rules holds the rules every agent follows. Both change only by pull request. Each agent page on this site shows its rules file. Tests /tests holds the Playwright and axe checks. Audit runs them on every new or changed prototype."},{"title":"Artifacts and templates","url":"artifacts-and-templates/index.html","description":"Seven artifacts carry the work between agents. Each has one writer, one home, and named readers.","text":"The seven artifacts Artifact Written by Where it lives Read by Product pack Archive, with Laurence's approval Repo /context , copy in SharePoint All agents UX brief Brief SharePoint, linked on the design item Insight, Map, the PM Discovery summary Insight and Map SharePoint, summary copy in the repo Range, Critic, Build, Observe Decision log Archive, from gate outcomes Repo /context All agents, Jim, PMs Extensions register Extend and Build Repo /context Align, Audit, Relay Migration map Archive Repo /context Build, Align Agent definition Laurence Repo /agents The agent itself Where each one comes from Product pack : one per product, including its styling mode. A change is merged in the repo, then Archive updates the SharePoint copy. Laurence or Jon approves every change. UX brief : Brief writes it from the template once the feature has everything it needs, and links it on the design item. Discovery summary : Insight lists every source, anonymises personal details and groups findings into themes with evidence. On Discovery approved, Archive writes the repo copy to /context/discovery/ feature .md . Decision log : after each gate, Archive adds the decision to decision log.md from the gate notes. Extensions register : one entry per extension, with a Figma half and a code half. Agent definition : one file per agent in /agents , changed by pull request. Templates The agents work from shared templates. These are named in the agents' rules: UX brief template , used by Brief Extensions register template , used by Extend and Build Prototype build spec , read by Build Greybox stylesheet greybox.css , the only styling Range may use To come: The template contents, each with a copy button, will be added from the source doc."},{"title":"Azure DevOps","url":"azure-devops/index.html","description":"Design has its own board in Azure DevOps. Each design item links into the product feature above it and the engineering stories after it.","text":"How the work items link The design board The UX design board belongs to the UX team. Its stage statuses trigger the agents, so the board is how the workflow runs, not just a record of it. The design item There is one design item per feature. UX owns it and its columns. Its parent is the product feature. It is the predecessor of the engineering stories. It links to the SharePoint UX brief, the Figma file and the prototype. Agents post their outputs on it: the Range index, the Critic review, Align and Audit reports, and the prototype preview. Fields Before design starts, Brief checks the feature has these fields complete. If any are missing, it comments the gaps on the feature and stops. Problem Outcome Products Users Priority Quarter Constraints The feature also carries a Design status field. The design item's status rolls up to it, and Relay sets it to Handed over. Columns The design item moves through the stage statuses: See the workflow /workflow/ for what each status starts. Links to product and engineering The portfolio epic is the parent of the feature. Both belong to product. The feature is planned per quarter and triggers design when it is set to Ready for design. Engineering stories are ready only when design is Handed over. Relay adds a linked UX section to each story, with UX acceptance criteria, Figma and prototype links, and sets the predecessor link. Relay never changes engineering statuses or the PM's story text. Swimlanes and Delivery Plans To come: How the design board uses swimlanes, and how design items appear in Delivery Plans, will be added from the source doc."},{"title":"Working with product","url":"working-with-product/index.html","description":"Product and UX meet through a contract, not through each other's agents.","text":"The contract Product management owns UX owns Epics and features Design items and the design board Outcomes and criteria The UX brief and success measures Story writing agents UX agents and templates Quarterly priorities UX sprint capacity Two things cross between them: Ready for design , from product to UX, and UX outputs on stories , from UX to product. What we consume A feature set to Ready for design . The status is the trigger. The feature fields: problem, outcome, products, users, priority, quarter and constraints. Brief will not start until they are complete. The feature's stories, SharePoint specs and related Teams and Outlook threads, which Brief reads. The PM's confirmation of scope on the UX brief. What we publish The UX brief and draft success measures, linked on the design item. The Design status field on the feature, which shows where design is. At handoff, a linked UX section on each engineering story: UX acceptance criteria Given, When, Then , Figma and prototype links, and the predecessor link. After release, a check by Relay of the build against the design, and of LogRocket data against the success measures. Working alongside other agents Product has its own agents, including story writing agents. Ours never do their work or change theirs: Brief never rewrites the PM's feature or stories. Relay never edits the PM's story text and never changes engineering statuses. Relay never hands over anything that has not been signed off. If a feature is missing fields, Brief comments the gaps on the feature and stops. It does not fill them in. Where product joins in The PM confirms scope on the UX brief. The PM sits on both gates, with designers and an engineer. PMs read the UX brief and the decision log."},{"title":"Set-up guides","url":"setup-guides/index.html","description":"Set up the foundations first, then connectors and access, then the agents. Each guide builds on the one before.","text":"The order 1. Foundations : the repo and its locks, product packs, the design board and the SharePoint site. 2. Connectors and access : Figma MCP write for Extend, read and Code Connect for the others , LogRocket MCP, Lucid MCP, the Azure DevOps connector and the legacy Figma libraries. 3. Agents , by stage. The guides To come: The nine set up guides will be added from the source doc. Each one will have its own page, with numbered steps, code to copy, links to the documentation and videos, and your progress through the nine. Adding a guide Each guide is a Markdown file in agentic/content/guides/setup guides/ , named with its position, for example 03 figma mcp.md . The site numbers them, shows progress and links each one to the next. markdown \"Guide file template\" title: Connect Figma MCP lead: One sentence on why this matters. Steps 1. First step. 2. Second step. Docs Link text https://example.com/docs Watch Video title https://example.com/video"},{"title":"How-to guides","url":"how-to-guides/index.html","description":"Short guides for the jobs that come up again and again.","text":"Guides"},{"title":"Governance and rollout","url":"governance/index.html","description":"People own every decision. This page says who owns what, how work is reviewed and how we will know the workflow is working.","text":"Ownership What Owner The workflow and this site Laurence Abbott backup: Jon Agent definitions in /agents Laurence, changed by pull request Rules in /.cursor/rules Changed by pull request Product packs Laurence or Jon approves every change Buckholt core and legacy CSS Their owners; no agent writes to them Direction and scope The shortlist gate: designers, PM, engineer Design sign off The sign off gate: designers, PM, engineer Reviews Every agent stops at a human check. The main ones: Designers validate Insight's insights and approve Map's flows and worklist. Designers review every Extend batch. Designers decide which of Align's issues are mistakes and which are deliberate. Designers check Build's prototypes for fidelity and behaviour. Audit blocks testing when it finds a critical accessibility issue. Designers review before Relay hands over; engineering reviews feasibility. Each agent's page lists its human gate. Keeping the system healthy Archive runs a weekly check and posts a health report. It flags: product packs older than 90 days extension register entries missing their Figma or code half gates with no decision logged It also tracks daily Figma MCP calls and warns the team at 80% of the limit. After each gate, Archive adds the decision to the decision log. Measures Every feature has success measures. 1. Brief drafts them in the UX brief. 2. Insight pulls LogRocket baselines for them: completion, drop off, errors and rage clicks on the flows in scope. 3. Observe compares test results with the baselines. 4. After release, Relay compares LogRocket data with the success measures and posts the report. To come: Measures for the workflow itself, such as time from Ready for design to Handed over, will be added from the source doc. Rollout phases To come: The rollout phases will be added from the source doc. Open questions These came up while building this site: The /agents rules files do not exist yet. Until they do, each agent page shows rules generated from agents.json . Buckholt has four expressive colour families for six stages, so Handoff shares Prototype's colour. Is that acceptable? Two of Buckholt's light expressive Tags are below WCAG AA contrast. This site darkens the label as an extension; the finding should go to the Buckholt owners. Where should the extensions register live: /context , as the brief says, or with each product?"},{"title":"Run a feature end to end","url":"how-to-guides/run-a-feature-end-to-end/index.html","description":"From Ready for design to Handed over, on the full route.","text":"Before you start The feature needs its fields complete: problem, outcome, products, users, priority, quarter and constraints. Each product it touches needs a product pack. Discover 1. Product sets the feature to Ready for design. Brief checks the fields, finds the product packs, chooses the route and writes the UX brief. 2. The PM confirms scope. A designer moves the status to Discovery. 3. Insight synthesises research and pulls LogRocket baselines. Designers validate the insights. 4. Map maps the journeys and writes the screen inventory and design worklist. Designers approve, then move the status to Discovery approved. 5. Archive writes the discovery summary into the repo. Explore 1. Range builds 5 to 10 greybox directions in the sandbox and posts an index. 2. Critic stress tests each direction and recommends up to three. 3. Shortlist gate. Designers, the PM and an engineer agree direction, scope and early feasibility. Status: Shortlist agreed. Design 1. Designers make the anchor screens in Figma. The status moves to Anchors ready. 2. Extend builds out the remaining screens in batches of up to five. Designers review every batch. 3. Align checks each batch for consistency. Designers decide what to fix. 4. Design sign off gate. Designers, the PM and an engineer agree the design meets the success measures and is buildable. Status: Design signed off. Prototype and test 1. Build builds the prototype in the product's styling mode and posts the preview. 2. Audit runs axe, keyboard, contrast and Buckholt checks. A critical accessibility issue blocks testing. 3. Observe drafts the test plan. Designers run the sessions; Observe tags the recordings and writes findings. 4. Findings go back to Extend or Build until designers mark testing complete. Hand over 1. Relay writes the specs and UX acceptance criteria and adds them to each engineering story. 2. Relay sets Design status on the feature to Handed over. 3. After release, Relay checks the build against the design and the success measures, and posts the report."},{"title":"Fast-track a change","url":"how-to-guides/fast-track-a-change/index.html","description":"Go from Discover straight to Design when a change is small and well understood.","text":"When a change can be fast tracked Brief chooses the route and records why. A change can take the fast track only when all three are true: there is no new flow no new research is needed only existing screens change If any one is false, the feature takes the full route. What is skipped On the fast track, the feature goes from Discover straight to Design. Explore is skipped: Range runs only on full route items, so there are no sandbox explorations, no Critic review and no shortlist gate. What is not skipped The UX brief, discovery and the design worklist. The design sign off gate, with designers, the PM and an engineer. Prototype and handoff, including Audit's checks. Handoff by Relay. To come: Whether fast tracked changes need new anchor screens, and any other differences, will be added from the source doc."},{"title":"Add a non-Buckholt component","url":"how-to-guides/add-a-non-buckholt-component/index.html","description":"When Buckholt has no component for what the design needs, add it as a flagged, registered extension.","text":"Check Buckholt first Look in Buckholt core, its HTML and CSS reference and its documentation. Only build an extension when nothing there fits. Then check the extensions register: if an entry already exists, reuse it. In Figma Extend builds the component on the Extensions page with an x/ prefix and adds a register entry. Library components are always used as instances; they are never detached or changed. In code Build builds it in /extensions/ product / : 1. Use Buckholt tokens CSS custom properties only. No hard coded colours, spacing, radii or fonts. 2. Prefix every class with x . 3. Add data buckholt=\"false\" to the component's root element. 4. Add or complete the register entry, so it has both its Figma half and its code half. css \"Tokens only\" .x example { padding: var padding 05 ; border: var border width solid var ui border 02 ; border radius: var border radius md ; color: var text primary ; } Checks Align checks that extension components are flagged and register entries have both halves. Audit flags hard coded values and x components with no register entry. Relay maps each element to a Storybook component or a flagged extension at handoff."},{"title":"Onboard a legacy product","url":"how-to-guides/onboard-a-legacy-product/index.html","description":"Bring a product that is not on Buckholt into the workflow without changing how it looks.","text":"What a legacy product needs Captured legacy CSS in /legacy/ product . It is locked like Buckholt: no agent writes to it, and changing it needs the owner's approval. A product pack that records the product's styling mode: Legacy or Hybrid. Its legacy Figma library , connected so designers can make anchor screens and Extend and Align can use it. A migration map in /context , which Archive keeps and Build and Align read. How the agents treat it Brief stops if a product pack is missing. Build uses the legacy CSS in Legacy mode. In Hybrid mode it follows the pack and labels every Buckholt element. Align flags styling mode mismatches, such as Buckholt parts in a legacy screen. Audit checks markup against the legacy CSS instead of the Buckholt reference. To come: The step by step onboarding procedure will be added from the source doc."},{"title":"Update a product pack","url":"how-to-guides/update-a-product-pack/index.html","description":"Change a product pack in the repo, get it approved, and Archive updates the SharePoint copy.","text":"Steps 1. Change the pack in /context on a branch and open a pull request. 2. Laurence or Jon reviews and approves it. Every product pack change needs that approval. 3. Merge the pull request. 4. Archive updates the pack's SharePoint copy so the two match. What Archive never does Change a pack without approval. Delete history. Keeping packs current In its weekly check, Archive flags packs older than 90 days. Every agent reads the product pack, so an out of date pack affects every feature for that product."},{"title":"Extensions on this site","url":"extensions/index.html","description":"","text":"extensions agent tag code block diagram contrast"}];
